CASE = [
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> The Rowlatt Act of 1919 was hurried through the Imperial Legislative Council despite the united opposition of the Indian members. It gave the government enormous powers to repress political activities and allowed detention of political prisoners without trial for two years. Gandhiji launched a nationwide satyagraha against it. On 13 April 1919, a large crowd gathered in the enclosed ground of Jallianwala Bagh, Amritsar. "
   r"(i) What power did the Rowlatt Act give the government? (ii) Who ordered the firing at Jallianwala Bagh? (iii) Why had many villagers gathered in Amritsar that day? (iv) How did the people of Punjab and other parts of India respond to the incident?",
   r"(i) To detain political prisoners without trial for two years (1). (ii) General Dyer (1). (iii) To attend the annual Baisakhi fair (and some came to protest the government's repression) (1). (iv) Crowds took to the streets in many north Indian towns, with strikes, clashes and attacks on government buildings; the government responded with brutal repression (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> In Awadh, peasants were led by Baba Ramchandra, a sanyasi who had earlier been to Fiji as an indentured labourer. The movement was against the talukdars and landlords who demanded exorbitantly high rents and various cesses. Peasants had to do begar. In October 1920 the Oudh Kisan Sabha was set up, headed by Jawaharlal Nehru, Baba Ramchandra and a few others. "
   r"(i) Who led the peasants of Awadh? (ii) Name the organisation set up in October 1920. (iii) Mention any two demands of the Awadh peasants. (iv) Why did the peasants' movement sometimes go beyond the Congress's programme?",
   r"(i) Baba Ramchandra (1). (ii) Oudh Kisan Sabha (1). (iii) Reduction of revenue, abolition of begar and social boycott of oppressive landlords (any two) (1). (iv) Peasants understood Gandhiji's message in their own way and attacked talukdars and looted bazaars, which was against the non-violent approach of the Congress (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> On 31 January 1930 Gandhiji sent a letter to Viceroy Irwin stating eleven demands. Irwin was unwilling to negotiate, so Gandhiji started his famous salt march accompanied by 78 of his trusted volunteers. The march was over 240 miles, from Sabarmati to the Gujarati coastal town of Dandi. The volunteers walked for 24 days. "
   r"(i) How many volunteers accompanied Gandhiji on the march? (ii) Where did the march end? (iii) Which movement began with the breaking of the salt law? (iv) Why was the salt law a suitable target for the movement?",
   r"(i) 78 (1). (ii) Dandi (1). (iii) The Civil Disobedience Movement (1). (iv) Salt was an essential item used by everyone, and the British monopoly and tax on it affected all classes, so the protest could unite the whole country against colonial rule (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
]

